A couple of months before the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a> came out, Samsung launched the Galaxy Z Fold8. It's Samsung's widest and shortest foldable to date, with Samsung adopting an aspect ratio closer to the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;'s than its previous foldable smartphones. Since the Z Fold8 was clearly designed to compete with the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, we thought we'd compare the two devices.<br/>
<br/>
<div class="center-wrap"><iframe title="iPhone Duo vs Galaxy Z Fold 8: Which Foldable Should You Buy?" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/SuMoG9ac0ak" width="560" height="315" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen"></iframe><em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/user/macrumors?sub_confirmation=1">Subscribe to the MacRumors YouTube channel</a> for more videos.</em></div><br/>
The Galaxy Z Fold8 and &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; are similar in size, but the Fold8 is noticeably lighter. It's about 50 grams lighter than the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, and it makes a difference when you're holding it. It's lighter because it's thinner than the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; when folded and unfolded. The Z Fold8 is 6mm taller and around 3mm narrower, though both have a 7.6-inch inner display.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; has a heftier feel and a more premium build, plus it has rounded corners that feel nicer in the hand than the Z Fold8's sharper, rectangular corners.<br/>
<br/>
Apple used a matte nano-texture display for the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, and that's likely something we're going to see Android smartphone makers imitate in their future foldables. The matte display goes a long way toward minimizing the visibility of the crease. Apple also put the camera on the inner display under the screen, which is something Samsung experimented with in earlier models and then abandoned.<br/>
<br/>
Under-display front cameras are generally lower resolution than standard cameras, and there have been complaints about the quality of the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;'s inner front camera. The Z Fold8 has a more traditional hole-punch camera that's better quality.<br/>
<br/>
Both smartphones use fingerprint recognition rather than facial recognition, but Samsung has long offered a face unlock feature that uses the front-facing camera. Apple just this week adopted a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/16/iphone-duo-face-unlock/">similar face unlock option</a>, and it's coming in iOS 27.3. Face unlock is less secure than the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/mask-face-id/">Face ID</a> system used for Apple's other iPhones, but it is more convenient than <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/touch-id/">Touch ID</a> for some users.<br/>
<br/>
Neither smartphone has a telephoto camera, so both Apple and Samsung compromised on camera quality. The &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; has a 48-megapixel Main camera and a 48-megapixel Ultra Wide, while the Z Fold8 has 50-megapixel wide and ultra wide cameras. The main difference in image quality is the computational processing each smartphone offers, so which one is "better" comes down to your preference for white balance, saturation, tone, and similar factors.<br/>
<br/>
The Z Fold8 supports 8K video recording and reverse wireless charging for accessories, plus it has a physical SIM while the Duo only has an eSIM. Samsung's smartphone is limited to IP48 dust and water resistance, while the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; has IP68. Samsung uses the Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5, and the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; has Apple's A20 Pro chip. The other big difference is stylus support. The &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; works with the USB-C <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-pencil/">Apple Pencil</a>, but the Z Fold8 doesn't work with Samsung's S Pen options.<br/>
<br/>
Since Samsung has been making foldable smartphones for years, developers have had more time to update their apps for the form factor. Most Android apps already work well, while we're still waiting for many developers to update their apps for the Duo. Samsung is also ahead on multitasking, with the option to use three apps at once and support for pop-up windows and a taskbar.<br/>
<br/>
Samsung's Galaxy Z Fold8 is slightly cheaper than the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, with pricing starting at &#36;1,899.99 while the Duo starts at &#36;1,999, but deciding between them will come down to your operating system preference and device ecosystem. If you're accustomed to iOS and have an Apple Watch, Mac, and other Apple devices, there's little reason to choose a Samsung smartphone. The same goes for Samsung users who have other Galaxy devices.<br/>
